## Where to find it

**Analytics** in the side drawer. It isn't a single dashboard — it's seven pages, each grouped around
one kind of decision rather than around one screen of the app:

| Page | What it's for |
| --- | --- |
| **Statistics** | Admissions, leavers and roll counts, counted from the student records. Listed first. |
| **Overview** | The starting point — the headline picture across the institute. |
| **Students & Risk** | Which individual students need attention. |
| **Academics** | Exam and assessment performance. |
| **Attendance** | Attendance together with punctuality. |
| **Payments** | Fees charged against fees actually collected. |
| **Operations** | How the institute is running day to day. |

**Payments** and **Operations** are admin-only. If you're a teacher or coordinator, those two won't
appear in your Analytics group — that's the intended access split, not a missing page.

## School Statistics

**Analytics → Statistics** counts students rather than scoring them. Choose a **Range** (*Last N years*)
and you get headline cards: **Students on roll**, **Admissions** and **Leavers** for the current year
(1 January to today), **Net change**, **Classes**, **Average class size**, **Teachers** and **Seats
filled**. Below them:

- **Admissions, leavers & students on roll** by **Month**, **Term** or **Year**, as a chart and a table
  with a total row. *Admissions* are students whose join date falls in the period; *leavers* are
  students marked as left with a leaving date in the period; *on roll* is students who had joined
  and not yet left on the last day of the period.
- **Students by grade** — students on the roll today by the grade of their current class, split by
  recorded gender (Male, Female, Not recorded) with classes and seats.

Students with no join date on record are not counted as admissions and are counted on the roll from
the start of the range; the page says how many there are. Term view needs term dates for each year;
years without them are listed and show no terms. See
[Set Holidays, Terms and Working Days](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-holidays-and-terms).

## How the Operations figures are worked out

Each figure on **Operations** has an info icon (hover, or tap on a phone) that explains it. In summary:

- **Class stability** starts each class at 100 and subtracts for attendance volatility over 60 days
  (40%), days its teacher was absent in the last 90 days (30%), substitutions per week (20%) and how
  much attendance drops when the teacher is away (10%). Higher is steadier.
- **Ledger mismatch** compares, for each closed day in the last 30, the net total saved at day close
  with the net recomputed from that day's ledger. A difference over 0.01 counts.
- **Data quality** counts active students (not alumni or left) with no RFID or *N/A*, active students
  with no tap in 30 days, card numbers tapped in 30 days that belong to no student, and days with
  taps but no daily attendance summary.
- **Voids and refunds** are measured against payments in the same 30 days; more than 5% of either is
  flagged.

## Narrowing to one class

Most pages accept a class filter, and the class you pick is carried in the page's own address. That
means a filtered view can be bookmarked or pasted to a colleague and it will open on the same class
for them — provided they have access to that page in the first place.

## Old links still work

The analytics section was reorganised from a single six-tab dashboard into these separate pages.
Older bookmarks and saved links still resolve:

- Numbered tab links (the original `?tab=` style) land on the matching page.
- The retired page names redirect to their new homes — *Exams* → **Academics**, *Financial* →
  **Payments**, *Punctuality* → **Attendance** (punctuality was merged in), and *Intelligence* →
  **Students & Risk**.

In every case the class you had selected is carried across, so you don't lose your filter. There's
no need to hunt down and update old links.

